 # Code Review
@@ -64,6 +65,43 @@ user commit unaware that open findings exist.
 - `--fix`: after reporting, apply fixes for the confirmed findings directly to the working
   tree, then re-run the affected checks (typecheck/tests) to confirm the fix didn't break
   anything. Do not silently fix — tell the user what was changed and why.
+- `--commit`: final step, after the report and any `--fix` — see Step 5.
+
+## Step 5 — Commit (only with `--commit`)
+
+Run this step **only** when `--commit` was passed explicitly to this invocation (in the
+command, e.g. `/code-review --fix --commit`, or in the skill's arguments). Never infer it
+from wording ("revisar antes de commitar", "ready to ship"), and never commit when running
+as a review subagent, even if asked. Without `--commit`, never commit.
+
+The project under review sets the rules: read its AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md for the checks it
+requires before committing and for its commit conventions. Commit only if **all** gates hold;
+otherwise make **no commit** and say which gate failed and why — the user decides what next:
+
+1. **There is something to commit.** Empty or trivial diff (nothing meaningful, e.g. only
+   whitespace/line-ending noise) → say there was nothing to commit.
+2. **No confirmed finding is left open.** Every confirmed finding was fixed — so `--commit`
+   without `--fix` only commits when there were no findings. List any uncertain finding you
+   left unfixed; those don't block.
+3. **The project's checks pass on the final tree.** Run, fresh and after the fixes, every check
+   the project requires before committing (e.g. typecheck + tests + build). If it declares
+   none, run its own typecheck/test scripts if it has them and name what you ran. Any failure
+   → no commit; quote the failing command and its error.
+
+How to commit:
+
+- Commit the whole reviewed diff — the original work plus your fixes — staged **by explicit
+  path**: `git add -- <paths>` then `git commit -m "<msg>" -- <paths>`, so anything else already
+  staged stays out. Never `git add -A`, `git add .` or `git commit -a`: other work may be in
+  progress in the same tree. Never commit `.env` files, credentials, keys or other secrets —
+  leave them out and say so.
+- Message in Conventional Commits (`type(scope): summary`), in the language of the repo's
+  recent history (`git log --oneline -10`), describing the change rather than the review.
+- No `Co-Authored-By` or other attribution trailer unless the project's `.claude/settings.json`
+  sets `attribution.commit` — ignore default commit templates that suggest one.
+- Current branch only: no push, no new branch, no `--amend`, no `--no-verify`.
+- Finish with the result: the commit's short hash and message (`git log -1 --format="%h %s"`)
+  and its file list (`git show --stat --format= HEAD`), or the reason no commit was made.
 
